  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging across the western CONUS will slide eastward \ntoward the Northern Rockies the next couple days, but flatten as it \ndoes so. The end result is for a continued dry pattern, with \ntemperatures continuing to trend warmer. Given the flattening of the \nridge, Friday and Saturday's forecast highs over the plains have \ntrended a bit lower over the past couple days, but still remain \nquite warm. \n\nThe zonal flow aloft more or less persists into next week, \nyielding little to no opportunity for precipitation across the \nregion. Temperatures look to fall a bit closer to average early \nnext week, though still remain on the warmer side of average. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nGusty winds over the plains today:  \n\nSurface low pressure exiting eastward this morning will result in \nsome breezy winds over the plains, largely near and east of US-87. \nThe probability for gusts to exceed 40 mph over the plains is less \nthan 10%, however. \n\nPrecipitation outlook:\n\nA few weak waves progressing through the zonal flow begin showing\nup late this weekend into early next week, but are wildly \ninconsistent from run-to-run and between guidance members. Hence, \nthe probability for precipitation in most areas remains quite low \nearly next week, largely 10% or less through Wednesday. -AM",
